{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Both can let the adviser recover prior waivers, but the governing agreement controls.\n\nUnder an ordinary expense-limitation agreement, the adviser generally may recoup waived or reimbursed expenses within a stated period (often three years), only if recoupment stays within both the expense cap in effect when the waiver occurred and the cap in effect when repayment is made.\n\nIn a unitary-fee arrangement, the adviser typically bears specified fund operating expenses in exchange for a fixed fee. Recoupment of a fee waiver is not automatic: if permitted, it depends on the contract’s terms and available headroom under the unitary-fee limit. It may not follow the ordinary agreement’s three-year/two-cap test.\n\nSo compare the specific prospectus and agreement—especially eligible expenses, recoupment window, and the applicable limit."}}
